    <!-- MARKUP VS MARGIN EXPLAINER -->
    <section class="content-section" id="markup-vs-margin">
      <h2>Markup vs. Profit Margin — The Difference That Costs Businesses Money</h2>
      <p>
        These two terms are frequently confused — and that confusion can silently
        destroy profitability. Here's the core distinction:
      </p>

      <h3>Markup: a percentage of cost</h3>
      <div class="formula-box">
        <code>Markup % = (Selling Price − Cost) / Cost × 100</code>
      </div>
      <p>
        A product that costs $50 and sells for $75 has a <strong>50% markup</strong>.
        Markup is always calculated relative to your <em>cost</em>.
      </p>

      <h3>Gross Profit Margin: a percentage of revenue</h3>
      <div class="formula-box">
        <code>Gross Margin % = (Selling Price − Cost) / Selling Price × 100</code>
      </div>
      <p>
        That same $50-cost / $75-price product has a <strong>33.3% gross margin</strong>
        — not 50%. If your business targets a 40% margin but you're quoting a 40%
        markup, you're leaving money on the table. Use the
        <a href="/business/markup-calculator">Markup Calculator</a> to convert between
        the two instantly.
      </p>

      <h3>Why Freelancers Underprice Themselves</h3>
      <p>
        Most freelancers calculate their rate by dividing desired annual income by
        2,080 working hours — and forget to account for self-employment tax (~15.3%),
        unpaid admin time, holidays, sick days, and business expenses. The result
        is an hourly rate that looks livable but leaves them below minimum wage
        after taxes. The <a href="/business/freelance-rate-calculator">Freelance Rate
        Calculator</a> builds all of these factors in automatically.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- PRICING CHECKLIST -->
    <section class="content-section" id="pricing-checklist">
      <h2>Business Pricing Checklist</h2>
      <p>Before you set a price for any product or service, confirm all five of these:</p>
      <ol class="hub-checklist">
        <li>
          <strong>Know your true cost.</strong>
          Direct costs (materials, labor) are obvious. But don't forget
          overhead allocation — rent, software subscriptions, insurance,
          and your own time. Divide total monthly overhead by units produced
          and add it to each item's cost.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Set a target gross margin, not a markup.</strong>
          Gross margin is what your P&amp;L speaks in. If you need a 40%
          gross margin to cover operating expenses and profit, use the
          <a href="/business/markup-calculator">Markup Calculator</a> to
          translate that into the correct selling price.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Check competitor pricing.</strong>
          Your math might say $120 but the market expects $89. Price
          elasticity matters — sometimes a lower margin at higher volume
          is more profitable than a premium price that stalls sales.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Account for payment terms and chargebacks.</strong>
          Net-30 clients cost you cash flow. Credit card processing fees
          (2%–3.5%) erode margin. Factor these into your effective cost
          before setting the final price.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Stress-test against your loan obligations.</strong>
          If you're carrying business debt, your gross margin must cover
          debt service <em>before</em> you pay yourself. Use the
          <a href="/loan-calculators/sba-loan-calculator">SBA Loan Calculator</a>
          or Business Loan Calculator to find your monthly debt service, then
          work backwards to the minimum revenue — and margin — you need.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

